Collectors — grouping and joining.

Reduce folds a stream down to a single scalar. But the most common thing you want at the end of a pipeline is a container — a list, a map, a report, elements bucketed by some key. That's what collect and the collectors factory are for. If reduction answers "what's the one number", collection answers "what's the assembled result".

Start with collect itself. It's a terminal that accumulates elements into a mutable container. You almost never write its low-level form by hand — instead, you pass a ready-made collector from the collectors factory. The everyday ones gather into collections. Collect with to-list gives you a list, though the newer stream-dot-to-list does the same. Collect with to-set gives you a set. And collect with joining, of comma-space, glues the elements into one string — Ada, Grace, Alan. Joining is the string specialist: give it a separator, a prefix, and a suffix, and it does in one pass what a manual string-builder loop would do.

Next, to-map — for building a lookup table. You turn a stream of objects into a map by giving two functions: one that extracts the key, one that extracts the value. So people dot stream, collect, to-map of person-name and person-age, gives you a map from each name to each age. One caveat worth knowing now: if two elements produce the same key, to-map throws. You must supply a third merge function to say how to combine collisions — for instance, keep the first.

Now the one you'll reach for constantly: grouping-by. This is the star of the section. It takes a classifier function and buckets every element by the key it returns, producing a map from key to a list of elements. It's the stream equivalent of SQL's group-by. So employees dot stream, collect, grouping-by employee-dept, gives you a map where engineering points to a list of those employees, sales points to another list, and so on.

But here's the real power: grouping-by takes an optional second collector that runs on each bucket. So you rarely want the raw lists — you want a number, or a shape, per group. You nest a collector inside the grouping. Grouping-by department, with counting, gives you how many employees per department. Grouping-by department, with averaging-double of salary, gives you the average salary per department. Grouping-by department, with mapping of employee-name into a list, gives you just the names in each department.

So counting, summing-int, averaging-double, mapping, joining — each one can serve as the downstream collector. Which means "group by department, then count, or average, or list the names" becomes a single declarative expression. And there's a special case: partitioning-by, which takes a predicate and splits the stream into exactly two buckets — true and false.

Why do collectors matter so much? Because a grouping-by with a downstream collector replaces a whole nest of map lookups, compute-if-absent calls, and accumulation loops — with one line that reads like the question you're asking. This is where streams stop being a nicer for loop and start being a small query language over your in-memory data. And it's the most-used corner of the entire Stream API in real code.
